---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { liveRecord as live } from "../lib/data.js";
---
<Base title="About" description="About this independent NBA forecasting project: what it predicts, how it is built, data sources and limitations." section="about">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head read">
      <p class="kicker accent">About</p>
      <h1>About The Hoop Ledger</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The Hoop Ledger is an independent project that predicts the final score of NBA games from information available before tip-off, turns each projection into a win probability, and grades every forecast once the game is over. The aim is a forecast that stands on basketball information alone and is honest about how well it works.</p>

      <h2>Why "ledger"</h2>
      <p>A ledger keeps every entry, wins and misses alike. Every game since 2022-23 has one forecast, from a model that had not seen it, graded against the final score. Two kinds of entry make up the record:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Live forecasts</strong>, saved by the daily pipeline before tip-off and never revised after. These are the real test, and there are only {live.n} of them so far ({live.w}–{live.l}).</li>
        <li><strong>Walk-forward forecasts</strong>, produced after the fact for every earlier game by retraining the model on prior seasons only. They show how the method holds up across thousands of games, but they were not published in advance.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The full record is on the <a href="/performance/">Performance</a> page and in the <a href="/forecasts/archive/">archive</a>; how the two are produced is on the <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> page.</p>

      <h2>What it predicts</h2>
      <p>For each game: a projected score for both teams, the projected winner, and the probability that the home team wins. It does not predict player statistics, and it does not make betting recommendations.</p>

      <h2>How it is built</h2>
      <p>A Python pipeline collects schedules, box scores and the official injury report, stores them in PostgreSQL, and runs gradient-boosted models built with scikit-learn. This website is a static site generated from a read-only export of that database, so it never connects to the database or runs the model itself. Details are on the <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> page.</p>

      <h2>Data and attribution</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Schedules, results and box scores: NBA Stats (stats.nba.com).</li>
        <li>Player availability: the official NBA injury report.</li>
        <li>Market lines, where shown: stored for comparison only; they are not model inputs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <p>This project is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to the National Basketball Association or any of its teams. Team names are used only to identify games. No team logos or player images are used.</p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>Forecasts are uncertain. The site is updated by hand, so results can lag. Forecasts are published for analysis and entertainment and are not betting advice. See <a href="/methodology/#limits">Limitations</a> for specifics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
